No fault is present now. End-to-end reachability from pc1 (195.11.14.2) to pc2 (200.1.1.2) is healthy: topology-wide probes show 0% loss and sub-millisecond RTTs both directions. The inter-AS link between router1 and router2 is up at Layer 1/2 on both sides (ethtool reports “Link detected: yes” on router1:eth0 and router2:eth0, 10Gb/s, Full Duplex). At Layer 3, BGP is established between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2): “show ip bgp summary” on both routers shows the single neighbor Up with prefixes exchanged (PfxRcd=1) and routes installed. Correspondingly, router1 has a BGP route to 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2, and router2 has a BGP route to 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1, confirming correct forwarding state.

Mechanism and localization: If the user observed a brief outage, it likely coincided with a recent BGP session flap or control-plane restart—the BGP uptime is only ~1m39s when checked—causing temporary loss during reconvergence. However, the physical interconnect (router1:eth0 <-> router2:eth0) and FRR daemons on both routers are currently healthy.

Root cause: No active cabling/interface failure; probable transient BGP session reset earlier or a momentary control-plane interruption.

Corrective fix: No restart is required. Recommend monitoring bgpd/zebra logs on both routers to identify any prior flap cause and ensuring FRR services are enabled and stable. If flaps recur, consider enabling BFD or reviewing timers, but do not bounce interfaces at this time.
